Z||000000||Greg Piet||11-11-1999||03:48 PM||||Ok when I try to time the 2.8 in diagnostic mode, I can get it to run at 10 btc, but it runs like garbage.   If I do it by ear, I can get her to run like a champ, but the timing mark is way off in diagnostic mode.  Could the pulley be 45 degrees out of phase?<P>I am also using and old timing light (non inductive) where i have a spring which connects between the cap and the plug wire and the timing light pickup then connects to the spring.  The official service manual tells me not to put a pinhole in the wire, but I dont see how  the spring could mess up the timing that much.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||88formula||11-11-1999||08:38 PM||||  If the engines #1 cylinder is at TDC dose the distributor line up with the #1 plug? Check this, the same thing happen to me as you dicribed, I couldn't time it properly because the distributor was installed wrong after an engine rebuild.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||88formula||11-11-1999||08:40 PM||||  If the engines #1 cylinder is at TDC dose the distributor line up with the #1 plug? Check this, the same thing happen to me as you dicribed, I couldn't time it properly because the distributor was installed wrong after an engine rebuild. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Tim||11-11-1999||10:38 PM||||The timing pointer on the cover maybe wrong for the damper. Which would be off by 60 degrees.  Check #1 TDC and see where the line is compared to the pointer.  If off paint new line.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Greg Piet||11-12-1999||01:26 AM||||I thought if the distributor was installed "wrong" that you would just have to keep turning the distributor untill when the mark on the pulley was lined up  at the 10 BTC mark, the rotor would be pointing to the #1 wire on the Distributor cap for the car to run.<BR>Ill have to check TDC manually, but if the pulley mark is accurate whats the deal with it running great then at about 50 deg btc and poor at 10?  (ive never seen this before in any car)||||reg||1
Z||000005||Tim||11-12-1999||11:27 AM||||If the marks are wrong it is not running at 50 degree BTDC.  The light will show 50 degrees because the mark is wrong.<P>GIGO(Garbage in = garbage out)||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||11-12-1999||01:10 PM||||The only 2 things that would make the timing marks off are the balancer is bad or the key sheared/missing. Balancers can go bad, or be damaged by people who don't handle them right. If the key breaks or is missing the balancer can creep out of position. It could also be the wrong balancer. A wrong one may work properly but have the timing marks cut for the pointer to be mounted diferantly.<P>The distributor doesn't care if its a couple teeth off as long as you can rotate the distributor body far enough to make up for it. ((Even my old 2.3l ford 4 cyl could have the dist roteted by as much as 90*. More than enough to make up for a tooth error.))||||reg||1
Z||000007||Carrolles||11-15-1999||07:53 AM||||Take a look at the harmonic balancer (damper) to see if is has failed.  It is made of a steel hub that bolts to the end of the crank, a rubber sleeve, and an outer steel ring that has the timing mark scribed on it.  The rubber sleeve is between the two matel parts.  These wear out eventually.  The outer sleeve usually starts to slip and will travel back towards the engine block.  The two metal parts should be in line .  Also the outer sleeve will sometimes turn on the inner hub causing the timimg mark to be inaccurate.  A new damper will cost around $55.00. <BR>During my engine replacement I noticed the timing mark bracket that is bolted to the front of the timing chain cover was cut pretty deeply by the damper rubbing on it.  The outer ring of the damper had moved back about 3/8" and the rubber sleeve was squeezed out about half way.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Tim||11-15-1999||11:36 AM||||Greg,<P>The timing mark bracket looking at the water pump for a Fiero is on the left side of the crank, on the right for RWD and FWD.  Zero degree is 60 degrees apart of these two brackets.  The damper MUST match the bracket you use for the timing mark to line up.  If the damper is not for the fiero and the bracket is for the fiero the timing mark would be off by 60 degrees.  Minus 10 correct initial timing equals 50 degrees.  Bring #1 cylinder to TDC(doesn't matter what stroke) and mark the damper at zero with a little paint, then put the light on.  I believe the Fiero is the only 2.8, et al that has the bracket left of the crankshaft, makes it easier to use a timing light.<P>BTW: 10 degrees BTDC initial at idle,  37 degrees total at 4000 RPM is the ideal according to the Chevy Power Book.||||reg||1
